Distributed General Intelligence: Instrument Validation, Feasible-Range Synergy Inference, and Design-Based Discrimination

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Abstract

This dissertation develops a formal framework for investigating whether general cognitive capabilities can emerge at the level of a multi-agent network when no individual agent possesses general intelligence independently. The central methodological claim is that the detection of distributed generality is not primarily a problem of constructing more powerful estimators, but of validating the instruments used to discriminate emergence from ensemble aggregation, task decomposition, and compute confounds. The framework is organized around four instrument-validation conditions that are logically prior to any evidential classification: (i) a feasible-range lower-bound test for partial information decomposition (PID) synergy, replacing the point-estimate gate of earlier formulations, in light of the documented non-uniqueness of PID atoms and the first-order magnitude of limited-sampling bias; (ii) a preregistered sham-detectability audit, motivated by paired-protocol evidence that format-matched placebos retain residual behavioral signal; (iii) dual Horvitz–Thompson and Differences-in-Neighbors estimation under cluster-randomized edge masking, with a preregistered divergence diagnostic for exposure-mapping misspecification; and (iv) a multi-rung positive-control ladder spanning exact parity, noisy parity, and distributed Gaussian estimation, with discrimination AUC reported separately at each rung. A correlational test of the framework’s central mechanism is proposed, in which the synergy score must exhibit incremental predictive validity for transfer to a procedurally novel task family after residualizing on individual capability and compute. The strongest defensible claim is stated conditionally: under the stated identification assumptions, after passing all four instrument-validation pre-tests, a network that passes the lower-bound synergy gate, exhibits a positive genuine edge-level contribution under both design-based estimators, and ranks above non-synergistic controls is a statistically distinguished candidate for further investigation of distributed generality. The framework does not claim to detect confirmed distributed general intelligence; it claims to provide a falsifiable instrument for discriminating candidate systems from known non-synergistic baselines.

Keywords: Distributed General Intelligence, Partial Information Decomposition, Feasible-Range Inference, Network Interference, Differences-in-Neighbors, Sham Controls, Discrimination AUC.

