1. The Measurement Problem and the Priority of Instrument Validation

1.1 From individual evaluation to distributed attribution

Contemporary artificial intelligence evaluation operates predominantly at the level of individual models. Multi-agent architectures complicate this picture: multiple agents can communicate, delegate, exchange intermediate results, use distinct tools, maintain separate memories, and coordinate actions. The central question of this dissertation is whether distributed interactions can generate general cognitive capabilities that are not explained by the capabilities of individual agents, by additional computation, or by conventional task decomposition.

The inequality

P(G) > \max_{i} P(A_i)

is insufficient evidence of emergent general intelligence, where G denotes the collective network and A_i an individual agent. An ensemble of identical models may outperform a single model through repeated sampling and answer selection. A specialized network may outperform every individual agent through task decomposition. A scientifically useful detection method must distinguish among individual intelligence, collective coordination, collective problem-solving, emergent capabilities, and distributed generality.

1.2 The thesis of this dissertation

The thesis defended here is that the principal obstacle to this discrimination is not the absence of formal machinery. It is the absence of instrument validation. The framework developed in earlier work imported validated operational methods—PID/TDMI synergy estimation, Horvitz–Thompson estimation under network interference, Hanley–McNeil AUC power analysis—and labelled their status correctly. But a method that is validated in one domain is not thereby validated in the domain of multi-agent language-model trajectories. The recent literature has documented failure modes in each of these instruments that can mimic the very phenomenon the framework is designed to detect. The contribution of this dissertation is to make those failure modes explicit, to convert them into preregistered diagnostic pre-tests, and to state the evidential claim only conditionally on their passage.

1.3 Evidential status labelling

Throughout, claims are labelled by formal status:

· [V] Validated: supported by a peer-reviewed or formally derived operational method that the framework applies in its validated domain.
· [E] Extension: a proposed method that adapts a validated technique to the multi-agent LLM domain; not yet validated in that domain.
· [R] Recommendation: a design principle or diagnostic heuristic that follows from the logic of the framework but lacks an established operationalization.
· [D] Derived: a formal result established within this dissertation, conditional on stated assumptions.

This labelling is itself a contribution to the rigor the framework claims to pursue. It is applied reflexively in Chapter 10, where the instruments are subjected to the same standard as the targets.

