2. Operational Definition and the Synergy Gate

2.1 Positive definition with a synergy gate

Distributed general intelligence is defined as a reproducible capacity of a multi-agent system to acquire, coordinate, and apply capabilities across multiple substantially different cognitive domains, including previously unseen generative procedures, where the resulting generalization (i) passes the synergy gate of §2.3, (ii) depends causally on edge-level interaction in the sense of Chapter 6, and (iii) cannot be reproduced by a compute-matched independent ensemble.

2.2 Three levels

· Level I — Individual Generality: measured directly.
· Level II — Collective Intelligence: coordination or improved collective performance, measured by \Pi against compute-matched baselines. Necessary but not sufficient for Level III.
· Level III — Distributed Generality: broad, transferable capabilities that (a) pass the synergy gate, (b) are causally attributable to edge-level interaction, and (c) survive T3 transfer.

2.3 The synergy gate

Let \mathbf{S}(G;\mathcal{T}) denote the partial information decomposition of time-delayed mutual information among agent states on task family \mathcal{T}. The synergistic component is I_{\mathrm{syn}}(G;\mathcal{T}); its bias-corrected estimator is \hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}.

The gate is a necessary condition: a network that fails \mathrm{Gate}_{\mathrm{syn}} can be classified S0 or S1 but never S2 or S3.

Formal status. [V] for the PID/TDMI estimator in the tested setting; [E] for the gate’s use as a necessary condition on multi-domain tasks; [R] for the threshold \varepsilon_{\mathrm{syn}}.

The empirical basis for the gate is the demonstration that multi-agent LLM systems can be steered from mere aggregates to higher-order collectives under appropriate prompting, and that PID/TDMI can measure this transition in a controlled guessing game. That demonstration establishes a proof of concept. It does not establish that the estimator is stable for open-ended multi-domain trajectories, and the remainder of this chapter and Chapter 3 address why.

2.4 Restricted-agent condition and capability certificates

Track A evaluates naturalistic networks. Track B evaluates controlled patchwork networks, subject to a certificate condition: each agent i must be measured across all domains d_j \in \mathcal{D}, and the per-domain performance vector \mathbf{c}^{(i)} must satisfy c^{(i)}_j \le \kappa_j for a preregistered ceiling vector \boldsymbol{\kappa}, with confidence intervals reported.

Formal status. [R] No published protocol for a per-domain capability-envelope certificate exists in the multi-agent LLM literature; the dissertation would develop it.

