3. Feasible-Range Synergy Inference and the Limits of Point Estimation

3.1 The bias problem is first-order

Let \hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) be the plug-in estimator and B_N(\mathbf{p}) = \mathbb{E}[\hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N)] - I_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}) the finite-sample bias. The bias-corrected estimator is

\hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\mathrm{corr}} = \hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) - \hat{B}_N,

where \hat{B}_N is a jackknife or bootstrap estimate. The systematic analysis of limited-sampling bias in discrete and Gaussian PID establishes that synergy is far more biased than redundancy, that “in practical applications the bias may be as large as the target information values,” and that this problem “can artificially skew comparisons of synergy and redundancy”. The bias is not a small-sample nuisance that vanishes under an effective-sample-size correction. It is a first-order quantity that can reverse the sign of the comparison the gate is designed to make.

The concentration bound of Chapter 3 is conditional on \hat{B}_N being close to B_N(\mathbf{p}). When B_N(\mathbf{p}) = \Theta(I_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p})), this condition is not a technical regularity; it is the substantive identification requirement. The framework must therefore report the sampling distribution of the corrected estimator, not only its point value.

3.2 Non-uniqueness of the PID

The framework’s gate is stated as a test on “the” synergy I_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}). This presupposes a unique decomposition. The decomposition is not unique. The multivariate PID literature resolves the two-source case with explicit closed-form formulas satisfying the full axiom set, but proves that for three or more sources “PID suffers from fundamental inconsistencies,” extending the known counterexample to “a comprehensive impossibility theorem showing that no lattice-based decomposition can be consistent for all subsets when the number of sources exceeds three”. The obstruction is representational rather than merely axiomatic: two systems can share identical atoms regardless of any axioms while having different mutual information.

Formally, let \mathcal{A} be the set of admissible PID atoms (\mathrm{Red}, \mathrm{Unq}_1, \mathrm{Unq}_2, \mathrm{Syn}) that sum to the joint mutual information and satisfy the non-negativity and self-redundancy constraints. The set of feasible synergy values is an interval

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}) = \left[ \underline{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}), \; \overline{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}) \right],

where the endpoints are attained by extremal PID measures within the admissible family. The gate should be defined as a lower-bound test:

\mathrm{Gate}_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\mathrm{LB}}(G) = \mathbf{1}\!\left[ \inf_{\pi \in \mathcal{P}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N)} I_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\pi}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) > \varepsilon_{\mathrm{syn}} \right],

where \mathcal{P}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) is the set of PIDs feasible for the empirical distribution. If the feasible interval includes zero, the gate returns inconclusive, not negative.

3.3 A convergent diagnostic

The co-information

C(S; R_1; R_2) = I(S;R_1) + I(S;R_2) - I(S;R_1,R_2)

is negative for synergistic systems and does not require a choice of PID lattice. It is not a synergy measure in the PID sense—it conflates synergy with source redundancy—but it is a model-free diagnostic of whether the system is in the regime where synergy is possible. The protocol should report both the feasible-range lower bound and the sign of the co-information. Agreement is evidence that the gate is identifying a stable phenomenon. Disagreement is evidence that the PID is not well-posed for the observed distribution, and the gate should abstain.

Formal status. [D] for the lower-bound test, conditional on the existence of a computable feasible set; [V] for the co-information as a Shannon identity; [E] for the concordance diagnostic as an operationalization.

3.4 What the gate does not claim

The lower-bound gate does not claim to measure a unique synergy value. It claims to identify networks whose synergy is positive under every admissible PID in the feasible set. This is a weaker and more defensible claim than point estimation, and it is the appropriate response to a quantity that is not point-identified.

