9. T3 Transfer as a Correlational Test of the Mechanism

9.1 The operational definition

T3 is defined as transfer to a preregistered procedural novelty benchmark: a task family that requires composing existing capabilities in a way not seen during training or T1/T2 evaluation. The benchmark is constructed by holding out a compositional procedure, not merely a task instance. The candidate network’s performance on T3 is measured after residualizing on individual capability \mathbf{C} and compute \mathbf{R}.

9.2 The correlational test

The framework’s core mechanism claim is that distributed generality is gated by synergy. The appropriate test is not a binary hurdle on T3 performance but a test of incremental predictive validity:

\Delta_{\mathrm{transfer}}^{(3)} = \mathbb{E}\!\left[ P(G, T_3) - P(B_{\mathrm{ens}}, T_3) \;\middle|\; \mathbf{C}, \mathbf{R} \right].

If the synergy score S_{\mathrm{syn}}(G) has incremental predictive validity for \Delta_{\mathrm{transfer}}^{(3)} after controlling for \mathbf{C} and \mathbf{R}, the mechanism claim is supported. If the synergy score is not predictive—if networks with high synergy do not show larger T3 advantages—the mechanism claim is falsified, even if some networks perform well on T3.

9.3 The parity-control prediction

The positive-adjacent parity controls should also be evaluated on T3. The null prediction is that their synergy score does not predict T3 advantage, because parity networks do not transfer to procedural novelty. If parity controls show the same synergy–T3 correlation as candidate networks, the framework’s mechanism claim is falsified. This is a strong test: it requires the framework to predict a null correlation for a class of systems where synergy is provable.

9.4 Honest limitation

The compositional generalization literature is developing—recent work addresses systemic failures of LLM agents in compositional settings through neuro-symbolic grounding and structured program representations—but no validated multi-agent LLM benchmark for procedural novelty in the specific sense required by T3 currently exists. The correlational test is a stronger design than a binary hurdle, but its power depends on the variance of the synergy score across the network class, which is not yet known.

Formal status. [R] for the T3 operationalization; [D] for the correlational design; [E] for the parity-control prediction as a falsification test.

