11. Conclusion

The possibility of intelligence emerging through distributed artificial systems creates a measurement problem that cannot be solved by examining individual agents alone. This dissertation has developed a framework that treats the problem as one of instrument validation: before asking whether a network exhibits distributed general intelligence, one must establish that the instruments used to answer the question are capable of discriminating the phenomenon from its confounds.

The framework imports validated operational methods where they exist—PID/TDMI synergy estimation, design-based causal inference under interference, Hanley–McNeil discrimination power analysis—and states explicitly where a method is an extension rather than a validated import. It replaces the point-estimate synergy gate with a feasible-range lower-bound test, in light of the documented non-uniqueness of PID atoms. It converts the sham control from an assumption into a preregistered audit, in light of evidence that format-matched placebos retain residual signal. It replaces the single HT estimator with dual HT/DN estimation and a divergence diagnostic, in light of the variance problem in dense graphs. And it replaces the single parity control with a multi-rung ladder, in light of the gap between exact and approximate synergy.

The strongest defensible claim is stated conditionally. A network that passes all four pre-tests, passes the lower-bound synergy gate, exhibits a positive genuine edge-level contribution under both design-based estimators, and ranks above non-synergistic controls is a statistically distinguished candidate for further investigation. It is not confirmed distributed general intelligence. The framework does not claim to have detected such intelligence; it claims to have constructed a falsifiable instrument for discriminating candidate systems from known non-synergistic baselines.

The residual gaps are empirical. The next phase of the programme must calibrate the instruments on a system where the ground truth is known and the message space is realistic. Until that calibration exists, the framework’s formal results remain conditional on assumptions whose failure modes are precisely the phenomena that the recent literature has begun to document. The framework’s closing principle remains the right one: Measure before classifying. Derive before asserting. Discriminate before detecting. Reject when the evidence requires it. The contribution of this dissertation is to apply that principle reflexively to the instruments themselves.

