Appendix A. Formal Status of the Synergy Estimator and the Feasible-Range Reformulation

A.1 The finite-sample bias of plug-in PID

Let S, R_1, R_2 be discrete random variables with finite alphabets of sizes |\mathcal{S}|, |\mathcal{R}_1|, |\mathcal{R}_2|. The plug-in PID estimator replaces the true joint distribution \mathbf{p} by the empirical frequency \hat{\mathbf{p}}_N over N samples. The bias of the plug-in joint information I(S; R_1, R_2) is of order (|\mathcal{S}| \cdot |\mathcal{R}_1| \cdot |\mathcal{R}_2|)/N, while the bias of single-source information I(S; R_j) is of order (|\mathcal{S}| \cdot |\mathcal{R}_j|)/N. Because the synergy atom is a difference between joint and union information, the synergy bias equals the difference between the joint-information bias and the union-information bias. The redundancy bias equals the difference between the sum of single-source information biases and the union-information bias. The asymmetry in the scaling laws—synergy bias growing with the product of all three alphabet sizes, redundancy bias growing at most with two—implies that synergy is systematically more biased than redundancy as the number of bins and sources increases.

The empirical literature confirms that the bias may be of the same order as the target information values. In one dataset, the uncorrected estimator would have concluded that synergy exceeded redundancy, whereas all bias-corrected estimates agreed that redundancy was higher. The implication for the synergy gate is structural, not incidental: if the bias is first-order, the corrected estimator \hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\mathrm{corr}} = \hat{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) - \hat{B}_N has a sampling distribution whose center depends on the behavior of \hat{B}_N. Lemma 1 of Chapter 3 conditions on \hat{B}_N being close to the true bias B_N(\mathbf{p}); when B_N(\mathbf{p}) = \Theta(I_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p})), this condition is not a technical regularity—it is the substantive identification requirement.

A.2 Non-uniqueness of the PID and the feasible set

The framework’s gate is stated as a test on “the” synergy I_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}). This presupposes a unique decomposition. The decomposition is not unique. A comprehensive 2026 review states that “there exists no unique solution constraining precisely how a PID should be constructed, leading to a multiverse of different formalisms with different mathematical commitments”. For two sources, the space of PIDs consistent with the standard Shannon constraints is one-dimensional; for three sources it is higher-dimensional; the dimension grows rapidly with the number of sources. MMI is the extreme measure on this space, but it is not privileged by the axioms.

Formally, let \mathcal{A} be the set of admissible PID atoms (Red, Unq_1, Unq_2, Syn) that sum to the joint mutual information and satisfy the non-negativity and self-redundancy constraints. The set of feasible synergy values is an interval

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}) = \left[ \underline{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}), \; \overline{I}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\mathbf{p}) \right],

where the endpoints are attained by extremal PID measures within the admissible family. The gate should be defined as a lower-bound test:

\mathrm{Gate}_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\mathrm{LB}}(G) = \mathbf{1}\!\left[ \inf_{\pi \in \mathcal{P}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N)} I_{\mathrm{syn}}^{\pi}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) > \varepsilon_{\mathrm{syn}} \right],

where \mathcal{P}(\hat{\mathbf{p}}_N) is the set of PIDs feasible for the empirical distribution. If the feasible interval includes zero, the gate returns inconclusive, not negative. This is the appropriate epistemic response to a quantity that is not point-identified.

A.3 A convergent diagnostic

The co-information C(S; R_1; R_2) = I(S;R_1) + I(S;R_2) - I(S;R_1,R_2) is negative for synergistic systems and does not require a choice of PID lattice. It is not a synergy measure in the PID sense—it conflates synergy with source redundancy—but it is a model-free diagnostic of whether the system is in the regime where synergy is possible. The protocol should report both the feasible-range lower bound and the sign of the co-information. Agreement (negative co-information and positive lower bound) is evidence that the gate is identifying a stable phenomenon. Disagreement (negative co-information but lower bound at or below zero, or positive co-information but positive lower bound) is evidence that the PID is not well-posed for the observed distribution, and the gate should abstain.

Appendix B. The Sham-Detectability Audit: Formal Construction and Power

B.1 The placebo is not neutral: empirical benchmark

The maximum-entropy sham distribution \mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{sham}} is constructed to preserve format and length while being orthogonal to the sender’s hidden state. It does not follow that the sham is behaviorally neutral. SwarmSafe-Delta, a paired executable protocol for LLM agent teams, reports the following violation rates across four models at N=4: real delivery 21.4\%, format-matched placebo 5.0\%, hidden delivery 1.0\%. The placebo effect is +4.0 percentage points relative to hidden delivery. The genuine message effect is +20.4 points. The sham subtraction therefore removes approximately 4/20.4 \approx 20\% of the total effect as protocol artifact. The resulting estimand \Gamma_e^{\mathrm{genuine}} is a lower bound on the semantic contribution, not a point estimate.

This benchmark is not a defect of the SwarmSafe-Delta protocol; it is a measurement of the residual signal in a carefully constructed placebo. The framework should adopt it as the calibration target for the sham audit: a sham that produces a behavioral shift of this magnitude is detectable in principle, and an audit that does not detect it is underpowered or using an insensitive discriminator.

B.2 The audit as a hypothesis test

Let D: \mathcal{M} \to \{0,1\} be a discriminator trained on surface features of genuine and sham messages, with no access to the sender’s hidden state. The null hypothesis is

H_0: \mathrm{AUC}(D) = 0.5,

against the two-sided alternative. The audit is preregistered with a power analysis. Using the Hanley–McNeil variance,

\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat{\mathrm{AUC}}) = \frac{\mathrm{AUC}(1-\mathrm{AUC}) + (N_+ - 1)(Q_1 - \mathrm{AUC}^2) + (N_- - 1)(Q_2 - \mathrm{AUC}^2)}{N_+ N_-},

with

Q_1 = \frac{\mathrm{AUC}}{2 - \mathrm{AUC}}, \qquad Q_2 = \frac{2\mathrm{AUC}^2}{1 + \mathrm{AUC}},

the required sample size for a one-sided test at level \alpha and power 1-\beta against a target \mathrm{AUC}_1 is

N = \frac{2(z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta})^2 \left[ \mathrm{AUC}_1(1-\mathrm{AUC}_1) + (Q_1 - \mathrm{AUC}_1^2) + (Q_2 - \mathrm{AUC}_1^2) \right]}{(\mathrm{AUC}_1 - 0.5)^2}

for balanced allocation. The Hanley–McNeil method is recommended specifically for hypothesis-testing designs and demonstrates superior operating characteristics for achieving adequate power.

A null result on an underpowered audit is not evidence of undetectability. The audit must report the minimum detectable AUC at the achieved sample size. If the minimum detectable AUC exceeds the benchmark implied by the SwarmSafe-Delta placebo (approximately 0.55–0.60 for a surface-feature discriminator), the audit is inconclusive and the sham cannot be used in the causal estimand.

B.3 The iterative constraint expansion

If the discriminator exceeds chance, the constraint set \{f_r\} is expanded to absorb the detected features, and the audit is repeated. The process terminates when either (i) the discriminator is at chance at the preregistered power, or (ii) no expansion achieves chance-level detectability. In case (ii), the genuine edge contribution estimand is not identified, and the framework must report this as an identification failure. The framework’s epistemic boundary #9 is thereby converted from a caveat into a falsifiable pre-test.

Appendix C. Dual Estimation for Edge Attribution: HT, DN, and the Divergence Diagnostic

C.1 The bias–variance tradeoff

The Horvitz–Thompson estimator \hat{\Gamma}_e^{\mathrm{HT}} is design-unbiased under the neighborhood SUTVA and strict positivity, but its variance can be exponentially large in the maximum degree of the interference graph. The Differences-in-Neighbors (DN) estimator is designed explicitly to mitigate network interference: compared to difference-in-means estimators it achieves bias second-order in the interference magnitude, while its variance is exponentially smaller than that of HT estimators. DN is not a competitor to HT; it is the estimator that makes edge-level attribution feasible when the HT variance is prohibitive.

Let \hat{\Gamma}_e^{\mathrm{DN}} denote the DN estimate of the direct edge effect. Under the clustered design of Chapter 6, both estimators target the same estimand \Gamma_e^{\mathrm{dir}} under the exposure mapping, but they have different finite-sample properties. HT is unbiased but high-variance; DN is biased at second order in the interference magnitude but low-variance. The bias of DN is O(d^2 \delta^2), where d is the neighborhood radius and \delta is the magnitude of interference; the variance is polynomial in d, not exponential.

C.2 The divergence diagnostic

Compute both estimators and their uncertainty intervals for each edge. Preregister the following interpretation:

1. Overlap: If the intervals overlap, report HT as the design-unbiased benchmark and DN as the practical point estimate. The edge-level contribution is supported under both estimators.
2. Systematic divergence: If the intervals diverge systematically across edges—HT positive and DN negative, or vice versa—the divergence is evidence that the d-hop exposure mapping is misspecified and that interference is non-local. This is a substantive finding about the network’s interference structure, not a nuisance. The edge-level attribution should be reported as lower-confidence, and the exposure mapping should be revised before FDR control is applied.

The divergence diagnostic does not require a ground-truth benchmark. It is a specification test for the exposure mapping, and its value is diagnostic rather than confirmatory.

C.3 Honest limitation

The DN estimator’s variance advantage is established on social networks and a city-level ride-sharing simulator, not on LLM communication graphs. Its application to the framework is an [E] extension, not a [V] import. The divergence diagnostic is a reasonable safeguard, but it has not been validated on the topologies the framework targets. The framework should state this explicitly and treat the DN results as exploratory until a calibration study on LLM communication graphs is available.

Appendix D. The Positive-Control Ladder: Exact Parity, Noisy Parity, and Gaussian Estimation

D.1 Exact parity as a non-circular positive control

The secret-sharing parity construction is non-circular: the ground-truth synergy is provable by construction, independent of the detector’s feature extraction pipeline. The connection between PID and secret sharing is established in the literature: an authorized set should have only synergistic information about the secret when all elements of the set are considered, and any shared-secret scheme yields a PID with a single non-negative atom. For the XOR/parity circuit, the Williams–Beer decomposition yields \mathrm{Syn} = H(Y) > 0, and masking any critical-path edge collapses reconstruction at the consensus node.

D.2 Noisy parity as a continuous benchmark

Exact parity is a point in the space of synergistic architectures. The next rung is noisy parity: each input bit is flipped independently with probability \epsilon, or the transmitted message is corrupted with probability \epsilon. The noisy-XOR construction represents “partial synergistic closure”: it is weaker than the pure XOR witness but illustrates the same structural point. As \epsilon increases, the synergy degrades continuously toward zero. A detector that passes on exact parity but fails on moderately noisy parity has limited resolving power, and the failure should be reported as a finding, not hidden by aggregating across control types.

Formally, let X_1, X_2 \in \{0,1\} be independent uniform bits, Y = X_1 \oplus X_2, and let the observed sources be R_j = X_j \oplus E_j with E_j \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(\epsilon) independent. The PID of (R_1, R_2) about Y is not purely synergistic for \epsilon > 0; there is residual unique information and redundancy whose magnitudes depend on \epsilon. The detector’s discrimination AUC should be reported separately for \epsilon \in \{0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}. If the AUC at \epsilon = 0.10 is not significantly above 0.5, the framework’s claim is limited to near-exact synergy, and its language must be adjusted.

D.3 Gaussian estimation as an analytically tractable rung

The third rung is a distributed Gaussian estimation task. Agents j = 1,\dots,k observe R_j = \sqrt{\rho} \, S + \sqrt{1-\rho} \, E_j, where S \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) is a latent target and E_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) are independent noise terms. The joint distribution is Gaussian, and the PID can be computed analytically from the covariance matrix. This provides a continuous, non-binary benchmark where the ground-truth synergy is a known function of (\rho, k). The Gaussian PID is also subject to bias, but the bias structure is analytically tractable and can be corrected using the exact expressions available for the Gaussian joint information.

D.4 Honest limitation

The noisy-parity and Gaussian rungs are principled designs, not established instruments. Whether passing the ladder predicts sensitivity to realistic LLM synergy is not known. The ladder is a calibration strategy, and its validation is an empirical task for the next phase of the programme.

Appendix E. T3 Transfer as a Correlational Test of the Mechanism

E.1 The operational definition

T3 is defined as transfer to a preregistered procedural novelty benchmark: a task family that requires composing existing capabilities in a way not seen during training or T1/T2 evaluation. The benchmark is constructed by holding out a compositional procedure, not merely a task instance. The candidate network’s performance on T3 is measured after residualizing on individual capability \mathbf{C} and compute \mathbf{R}.

E.2 The correlational test

The framework’s core mechanism claim is that distributed generality is gated by synergy. The appropriate test is not a binary hurdle on T3 performance but a test of incremental predictive validity:

\Delta_{\mathrm{transfer}}^{(3)} = \mathbb{E}\!\left[ P(G, T_3) - P(B_{\mathrm{ens}}, T_3) \;\middle|\; \mathbf{C}, \mathbf{R} \right].

If the synergy score S_{\mathrm{syn}}(G) has incremental predictive validity for \Delta_{\mathrm{transfer}}^{(3)} after controlling for \mathbf{C} and \mathbf{R}, the mechanism claim is supported. If the synergy score is not predictive—if networks with high synergy do not show larger T3 advantages—the mechanism claim is falsified, even if some networks perform well on T3.

E.3 The parity-control prediction

The positive-adjacent parity controls should also be evaluated on T3. The null prediction is that their synergy score does not predict T3 advantage, because parity networks do not transfer to procedural novelty. If parity controls show the same synergy–T3 correlation as candidate networks, the framework’s mechanism claim is falsified. This is a strong test: it requires the framework to predict a null correlation for a class of systems where synergy is provable, which is a non-trivial constraint.

E.4 Honest limitation

The compositional generalization literature is developing, but no validated multi-agent LLM benchmark for procedural novelty in the specific sense required by T3 currently exists. The operationalization is a recommendation [R], not a validated instrument [V]. The correlational test is a stronger design than a binary hurdle, but its power depends on the variance of the synergy score across the network class, which is not yet known.

Appendix F. The Revised Evidential Claim and Its Preconditions

The strongest defensible claim of the framework is conditional on four instrument-validation pre-tests. Let \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{PID}} denote the feasible-range lower-bound test of Appendix A, \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{sham}} the sham-detectability audit of Appendix B, \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ladder}} the multi-rung discrimination ladder of Appendix D, and \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{dual}} the dual HT/DN estimation with divergence diagnostic of Appendix C. The claim is:

Under the stated identification assumptions, after passing \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{PID}} (the feasible-range lower bound exceeds \varepsilon_{\mathrm{syn}} and the co-information diagnostic is concordant), after passing \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{sham}} (the maximum-entropy control is not discriminable from genuine messages at the preregistered power), after passing \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ladder}} (the discrimination AUC is significantly above 0.5 at the lowest rung the detector claims to handle, including noisy parity), and after \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{dual}} (the genuine edge contribution is positive under both HT and DN estimation, with no systematic divergence indicating exposure-mapping misspecification), a network that passes the synergy gate, exhibits a positive genuine edge-level contribution after FDR control, and ranks above non-synergistic controls is a statistically distinguished candidate for further investigation of distributed generality.

This claim is weaker than the original in the sense that it adds preconditions. It is stronger in the sense that the preconditions are falsifiable tests, not assumptions that are stated but not examined. The framework’s own principle—Discriminate before detecting—applied reflexively to its instruments, requires exactly this structure.

Appendix G. Residual Gaps and the Limits of the Present Evidence

Three gaps cannot be closed by the present literature, and the framework should state them without hedging.

G.1 No validated ground-truth synergy benchmark for open-ended multi-agent LLM tasks. The parity ladder and the Gaussian estimation task are calibration steps, but they are not standards for the gate. The PID bias-correction results are established on neural data and simulated systems; whether the bias structure transfers to LLM state trajectories is an open empirical question.

G.2 The positive-control ladder is not validated. Noisy parity is a more demanding test than exact parity, but whether passing it predicts sensitivity to realistic LLM synergy is not known. The ladder is a principled design, not an established instrument.

G.3 The DN estimator has not been validated on LLM communication graphs. Its advantage is established on social networks and ride-sharing simulators. The divergence diagnostic is a safeguard, but it is not a substitute for validation on the target topologies.

These gaps do not invalidate the programme. They mean that the next phase’s most important work is empirical calibration, not further mathematical derivation. The framework has sufficient formal machinery; what it lacks is a calibration study on a system where the ground truth is known and the message space is realistic. Until that study exists, the derived results of Chapters 3 through 7 remain conditional on assumptions whose failure modes—PID non-uniqueness, sham leakage, narrow positive controls, exposure-mapping misspecification—are precisely the phenomena that the recent literature has begun to document.

